  "key_findings": {
    "hyperthermia_trap": "Heavy cutaneous coverings (ARCH-1, 75% coverage) induce catastrophic hyperthermia. In hot-dry and hot-humid climates, body temperature rises by +5.3 K within 2700 s, reaching the critical 39.5 °C threshold in just 940 seconds (~15.6 minutes). Without evaporative cooling, biological armor acts as a thermal trap.",
    "metabolic_mass_penalty": "ARCH-1's additional 14.8 kg mass and 2.4x internal joint work reduces delivered external work by 71.2% (from 88.9 kJ down to 25.6 kJ) due to severe energetic budget capping. Over 68% of available metabolic power is consumed merely moving the armor plates.",
    "climatic_divergence": "In temperate conditions, ARCH-4 (balanced FGM, 4.2 kg) maintains thermal stability (delta K = +0.69 K) while delivering 92% of baseline external work. However, in hot-humid conditions (35 °C, 80% RH), even ARCH-4 reaches hyperthermia (+3.8 K) due to atmospheric vapor pressure saturation.",
    "pareto_superiority_of_selective_defense": "ARCH-4 decisively dominates ARCH-1 across all physiological endurance axes, demonstrating that full-body bio-mineralization is evolutionarily and functionally non-viable for sustained high-intensity combat locomotion."
  }
